{
  "id": "timedelta-mode-precedence-ser-json-temporal-over-ser-json-ti",
  "title": "Timedelta mode precedence (ser_json_temporal over ser_json_timedelta) is resolved in one helper; keep all entry points using it",
  "kind": "rule",
  "answers": [
    "which wins, ser_json_temporal or ser_json_timedelta",
    "where is timedelta serialization mode resolved",
    "adding a new temporal config option to pydantic-core serializers"
  ],
  "body": "Invariant: for timedeltas, `ser_json_temporal` takes precedence when it is present in the config. Only when it is absent does the deprecated `ser_json_timedelta` apply. Other datetime types never look at `ser_json_timedelta`.\nWhere it's enforced: config dicts go through `TemporalMode::timedelta_from_config` in pydantic-core/src/serializers/config.rs. Both `SerializationConfig::from_config` and `TimeDeltaSerializer::build` (pydantic-core/src/serializers/type_serializers/timedelta.rs) call it.\nString args (`SerializationConfig::from_args`, used by `to_jsonable_python(timedelta_mode=..., temporal_mode=...)`) use a different check: temporal wins unless it equals the default `TemporalMode::Iso8601`, because a string arg can't tell 'unset' apart from 'iso8601'.\nIf you skip it: schema-based and inferred serialization disagree on timedelta output, or the deprecated setting leaks onto other types. Don't copy the `contains(\"ser_json_temporal\")` check inline again. Change the helper.",
  "applies": "Any change to temporal/timedelta serialization config resolution in pydantic-core",
  "tags": [
    "serialization",
    "timedelta",
    "config",
    "precedence",
    "pydantic-core"
  ],
